# msync() flushes whole pages of the mapped datafile, including the gaps
# between nodes inside the touched pages, which are never written/initialized.
# Such gaps are never read back, so the valgrind's complaint about the
# uninitialised msync() argument is a false positive.
{
   msync-uninit-page-holes
   Memcheck:Param
   msync(start)
   ...
   fun:osal_msync*
}

# The shell-scripted tests (the tools' golden-runners and friends) are
# executed by bash, which is instrumented by valgrind as a traced child.
# Bash leaks some of its own parse/execute structures at exit - not our
# business, but such reports would fail the test.
{
   bash-self-leaks
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:*alloc
   obj:/usr/bin/bash*
   ...
}

# The shadow-pages parked in the env's shadow_reserve (an interior-pointers
# chain) at the exit of a faulted actor, which leaves its environment open.
{
   faulted-actor-shadow-pages
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:malloc
   fun:page_shadow_alloc*
   ...
}

# The same shadow-pages, but for the LTO builds where the malloc() frame is
# merged into page_shadow_alloc itself (the suppression matcher walks the
# stack top-down, so the allocator's own frame must be pinned here).
{
   faulted-actor-shadow-pages-lto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:*page_shadow_alloc*
   ...
}

# A fork()ed child that dies by design - the crash/durability scenarios kill
# the child while it holds an open environment, and the fork-vs-locks
# scenarios (gh0329/gh0324) leave the child with an environment inherited
# from the multi-threaded parent - exits while its env's basal pools are
# still anchored: the mappings and the lck region keep interior pointers,
# but nothing will ever run mdbx_env_close() in that process. This is the
# scenario itself, not a leak (the same reason the smoke_fault framework
# substitutes txn_abort for SIGKILL under Valgrind, see
# tests/framework/test.c++). Valgrind classifies such blocks as "possibly
# lost"; the "definitely lost" ones stay reported (and failing) as they
# should. Two variants for the LTO/non-LTO stack shapes.
# Accepted risk: the pattern anchors on the allocation path, not on the
# fork context, so a hypothetical parent-side "possibly lost" leak of the
# basal pools after mdbx_env_open() would be hidden too - the definite
# kinds remain reported (and failing) either way.
{
   fork-child-drowned-env-pools
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:*alloc
   fun:*pnl_alloc*
   fun:txn_basal_create*
   fun:mdbx_env_open*
   ...
}
{
   fork-child-drowned-env-pools-lto
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:*pnl_alloc*
   fun:txn_basal_create*
   fun:mdbx_env_open*
   ...
}

# A fork() of a multi-threaded process inherits the live threads'
# TLS/DTV blocks; glibc releases them only when the threads themselves
# exit, and there are no threads in the forked child. A foreign-code
# artifact in the bash-self-leaks sense, not our allocation. The leading
# wildcard absorbs the allocator wrapper frames (glibc's rtld-malloc.h
# calloc and the like) that LTO inlining adds between them.
{
   fork-child-inherited-tls-dtv
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   ...
   fun:allocate_dtv
   fun:_dl_allocate_tls
   fun:allocate_stack
   fun:pthread_create*
}

# A fork() of a multi-threaded parent racing its own churn thread (the
# gh0329 scenario) can inherit a transiently-unanchored allocation: e.g. the
# DBI-name clone between its malloc() (dbi.c) and the hook into
# env->kvs[].name a few statements later. The parent owns and frees that
# block correctly; only the fork-made copy is stranded in the dying child,
# and a child cannot release its parent's mid-flight allocation safely.
# This is the fork contract, not a leak. The false-negative risk (a real
# leak of such a clone inside a forked child would be hidden) is accepted
# for this alloc path alone.
{
   gh0329-fork-inherited-dbi-name-clone
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:*alloc
   fun:*dbi_open_locked*
   ...
}
